<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2012_09841_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2012.09841_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2012.09841_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2012_09841_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "architecture for language tasks [ 74 , 57 , 58 , 5 ] 1 all positions. This makes them ineffective if a more holistic understanding of the input is required. Our key insight to obtain an effective and expressive model is that, taken together, convolutional and transformer architectures can model the compositional nature of our visual world [ 51 ] : We use a convolutional approach to ef- ciently learn a codebook of context-rich visual parts and, subsequently, learn a model of their global composit" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2012_09841_rag__chunk_013> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2012.09841_rag::chunk_013" ;
    kg:docId "2012.09841_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2012_09841_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "VQGAN 16 16 1024 7.94 10.54 VQGAN 16 16 16384 4.98 7.41 VQGAN 32 32 8192 1.49 3.24 VQGAN 64 64 & 32 32 512 1.45 2.78 This paper adressed the fundamental challenges that previously conned transformers to low-resolution images. We proposed an approach which represents images as a composition of perceptually rich image constituents and thereby overcomes the infeasible quadratic complexity when modeling images directly in pixel space. Modeling constituents with a CNN architecture and their compositi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_03334_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.03334_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2102.03334_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_03334_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "ViLT: Vision-and-Language Transformer Without Convolution or Region Supervision Wonjae Kim * 1 Bokyung Son * 1 Ildoo Kim 2 Visual Embedding Schema Abstract arXiv:2102.03334v2 [stat.ML] 10 Jun 2021 Region Feature (ViLBERT, UNITER, ...) Region Operations CNN Backbone Vision-and-Language Pre-training (VLP) has improved performance on various joint vision-andlanguage downstream tasks. Current approaches to VLP heavily rely on image feature extraction processes, most of which involve region supervisi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_03334_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.03334_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2102.03334_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_03334_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "be fed into VLP models, image pixels need to be initially embedded in a dense form alongside language tokens. Since the seminal work of Krizhevsky et al. ( 2012 ), deep convolutional networks have been regarded as essential for this visual embedding step. Most VLP models employ an object detector pre-trained on the Visual Genome dataset ( Krishna et al. , 2017 ) annotated with 1,600 object classes and 400 attribute classes as in Anderson et al. ( 2018 ). Pixel- * Equal contribution Current aflia" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_03334_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.03334_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2102.03334_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_03334_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Suhr et al. ( 2018 ) that all models with simply fused multimodal representation failed to learn NLVR2. Whereas all performant VLP models share the same textual embedder tokenizer from pre-trained BERT, word and position embeddings resembling those of BERT they differ on visual embedders. Still, in most (if not all) cases, visual embedding is the bottleneck of existing VLP models. We focus on cutting corners on this step by introducing patch projection instead of using region or grid features fo" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_12092_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.12092_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2102.12092_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_12092_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Zero-Shot Text-to-Image Generation Aditya Ramesh 1 Mikhail Pavlov 1 Gabriel Goh 1 Scott Gray 1 Chelsea Voss 1 Alec Radford 1 Mark Chen 1 Ilya Sutskever 1 Abstract Text-to-image generation has traditionally focused on nding better modeling assumptions for training on a xed dataset. These assumptions might involve complex architectures, auxiliary losses, or side information such as object part labels or segmentation masks supplied during training. We describe a simple approach for this task based " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_12092_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.12092_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2102.12092_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_12092_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "p ( y, z ) , which yields the lower bound 2. Method ln p , ( x, y ) E z q ( z | x ) ln p ( x | y, z ) Our goal is to train a transformer ( Vaswani et al. , 2017 ) to autoregressively model the text and image tokens as a single stream of data. However, using pixels directly as image tokens would require an inordinate amount of memory for high-resolution images. Likelihood objectives tend to prioritize modeling short-range dependencies between pixels ( Salimans et al. , 2017 ), so much of the mode" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_12092_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.12092_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2102.12092_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_12092_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "regarded as collectively approximating the gradient for the full parameter matrix. Figure 7. Human evaluation of our model (evaluated zero-shot without temperature reduction) vs prior work (DF-GAN) on captions from MS-COCO. In a best-of-ve vote, our models sample was chosen as the most realistic 90.0% of the time, and was chosen as the image best matching a shared caption 93.3% of the time. Figure 8. Zero-shot samples from our model on the CUB dataset. and we found that it includes about 21% of " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2102_12092_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2102.12092_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2102.12092_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2102_12092_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "a contrastive model specically trained for this task. We then sort the images in descending order by closeness to their nearest matches in the training data. After inspecting the results by hand, we determine the images to remove by manually selecting a conservative threshold designed to minimize the false negative rate. To a limited degree of reliability, we also nd our model to be capable of zero-shot image-to-image translation controllable by natural language (Figure 2d ). When the model is g" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2103_00020_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2103.00020_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2103.00020_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2103_00020_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision Alec Radford * 1 Jong Wook Kim * 1 Chris Hallacy 1 Aditya Ramesh 1 Gabriel Goh 1 Sandhini Agarwal 1 Girish Sastry 1 Amanda Askell 1 Pamela Mishkin 1 Jack Clark 1 Gretchen Krueger 1 Ilya Sutskever 1 Abstract Task-agnostic objectives such as autoregressive and masked language modeling have scaled across many orders of magnitude in compute, model capacity, and data, steadily improving capabilities. The development of text-to-text" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2103_00020_rag__chunk_009> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2103.00020_rag::chunk_009" ;
    kg:docId "2103.00020_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2103_00020_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "additional compute across all of width, depth, and resolution outperforms only allocating it to only 3. Experiments training as a transfer learning method to improve supervised ne-tuning, it also included an ablation study demonstrating that the performance of four heuristic zero-shot transfer methods improved steadily over the course of pre-training, without any supervised adaption. This analysis served as the basis for GPT-2 ( Radford et al. , 2019 ) which focused exclusively on studying the t" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2103_00020_rag__chunk_012> a kg:Chunk ;
