    kg:textSpan "comprises of text, images, videos, and audio clips. Reka Flash and Reka Edge were trained on approximately 5 trillion and 4 . 5 trillion extensively deduplicated and filtered language tokens, respectively. While the classification of corpora is not strictly defined to one class or category, approximately 25% of our pretraining data is code related, and 30% are STEM related. Approximately 25% of the data is web crawl. About 10% of our data has some relation to math. Overall mixture rates generall" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_12390_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.12390_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "2404.12390_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_12390_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "is one of the earliest holistic benchmarks containing multi-modal Yes/No questions on the defined visual perception and language Blink 5 reasoning tasks. MM-Vet [ 86 ] includes six sub-features from the previous datasets including recognition-focused questions, OCR, and math, providing a diverse while discrete evaluation set. MMBench [ 52 ] covers more subjects and provides a more robust circular evaluation setting. Seed-Bench [ 43 , 44 ] benchmark has a more diverse source of inputs, including " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_12390_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.12390_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2404.12390_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_12390_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "We reformat the claims into binary questions via GPT-3.5 [ 13 ]. Multi-view reasoning: This task is centered on evaluating the multi-view reasoning capabilities of Multimodal LLMs. The objective is to deduce the relative camera motion based on two images of an object captured from different viewpoints. Our data is sourced from the Wild6D dataset [ 88 ], which features videos of various objects recorded in diverse settings. We select two random frames from each video to calculate the relative cam" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_12390_rag__chunk_013> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.12390_rag::chunk_013" ;
    kg:docId "2404.12390_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_12390_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "perceiving, processing, and understanding complex visual and textual context. 4.3 Analysis Is dense captioning all you need for a multimodal LLM benchmark? To answer the question, we reduce multimodal benchmarks to a text-only problem. Specifically, we convert images into task-agnostic dense image captions with GPT-4V. The dense caption describes detailed information about the image and the visual prompts ( e.g ., where each circle is), using language. For each multimodal question, we prompt the" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_13013_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.13013_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2404.13013_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_13013_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "real-world applications like robotics, autonomous driving, and augmented reality. In light of the gap, one stream of research attempts to augment the LLM to directly output quantized object coordinates for localization [ 3 , 6 , 7 , 38 , 49 , 57 ] (Fig. 2(a) ). While this method is simple in design, the substantial computational demands of LLMs make it challenging to process high-resolution image inputs, which are essential for accurate localization. Besides, the nature of sequence outputs in LL" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_13013_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.13013_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2404.13013_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_13013_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to the magnitude as LLMs. While these works have exhibited impressive visual understanding capabilities, they are predominantly constrained to image-level tasks, such as image captioning and image visual question answering. This necessitates the research into region-level MLLMs, which unlock more nuanced and granular visual-language interactions. Region-level MLLMs. In pursuit of fine-grained and grounded image understanding, recent studies further integrate region-level data into the training o" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_13013_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.13013_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2404.13013_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_13013_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "their referring expressions, which is insufficient to thoroughly evaluate the MLLMs localization capability. Therefore, we further introduce LVIS-Ground, an object grounding benchmark converted from the LVIS [ 14 ] detection data. LVIS-Ground contains 4299 images covering 1203 categories of objects, with on average 3.7 target objects per image. Complementary to REC benchmarks, LVIS-Ground focuses on testing the models ability to locate multiple, diverse, and variably-sized objects. For more deta" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_13046_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2404.13046_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2404.13046_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2404_13046_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Our evaluation criteria encompass a variety of dimensions: comprehensive benchmarks [ 16 ], text-oriented Visual Question Answering (VQA) [ 17 , 18 ], general VQA [ 19 ], object hallucination [ 20 ], Referring Expression Comprehension (REC) [ 21 ], Referring Expression Segmentation (RES) [ 21 ], and medical VQA benchmark SLAKE [22]. We use the same data for each model. columns: vision_encoder | task | mmb_docvqa_chartqa_gqa_pope_rec_res_slake | clip_11 | col5 row: Image-text Contrastive; vision_" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2404_13046_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
